  "verbalization": "Epoprostenol is implicated in the pathogenesis of the common cold through a chain of indications that ultimately lead to the condition. The drug is associated with Allergic Rhinitis, a condition that is itself linked to the common cold via multiple pathways. Specifically, Epoprostenol is linked to Allergic Rhinitis, which in turn is connected to Chlorcyclizine and Pentoxyverine, both of which are indicated to cause the common cold. These relationships suggest a potential mechanism where Epoprostenol may influence immune responses or inflammatory processes that contribute to the development of the common cold. The evidence is consistent across three distinct paths, reinforcing the notion that Epoprostenol may play a role in the progression or manifestation of the common cold through its involvement in allergic rhinitis. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible link between Epoprostenol and the common cold, though further research is needed to elucidate the exact mechanism.",
